1
00:00:05,934 --> 00:00:08,119
Hello, I'm Sangsoo Jeong.

2
00:00:08,120 --> 00:00:12,200
This time, let's talk about surviving as a professional.

3
00:00:12,201 --> 00:00:15,560
Usually, a person who gets paid for their work is called a professional.

4
00:00:15,561 --> 00:00:20,440
Once drawing becomes your job rather than a hobby, of course a sense of responsibility comes with it.

5
00:00:20,441 --> 00:00:21,960
It should.

6
00:00:21,961 --> 00:00:26,920
Also, to keep from falling behind, you have to put in twice the effort of those who draw as a hobby.

7
00:00:26,921 --> 00:00:31,560
In other words, unlike when you draw as a hobby, the weight of responsibility and achievement grows.

8
00:00:31,561 --> 00:00:35,400
So some people say that drawing is most enjoyable when you leave it as a hobby.

9
00:00:36,245 --> 00:00:40,880
That's not a wrong thing to say, depending on what standard you think of "enjoyment" by.

10
00:00:40,881 --> 00:00:48,480
If you find it enjoyable to spend time drawing casually, then you'd keep it a hobby, but those of you watching this class probably won't.

11
00:00:48,481 --> 00:00:53,840
After all, this class isn't for people who draw as a hobby.

12
00:00:53,841 --> 00:00:57,120
Then responsibility and achievement would become important.

13
00:00:57,121 --> 00:00:58,720
Is that too much of a cliche?

14
00:00:58,721 --> 00:01:00,880
But there's no other way to put it.

15
00:01:02,280 --> 00:01:06,040
What is the responsibility of a professional who draws?

16
00:01:06,040 --> 00:01:16,360
Let's say A, who vaguely started drawing because she just wanted to get good at drawing, and B, who started drawing because he wanted to create game characters, are both working at the same company.

17
00:01:16,361 --> 00:01:19,000
A has no interest in the company's work.

18
00:01:19,001 --> 00:01:27,320
A wants to draw pretty, cool characters, but the higher-ups keep telling her to draw weapons and put together concept sheets.

19
00:01:27,320 --> 00:01:30,561
Annoyed by all of it, A does personal work on the sly at the company.

20
00:01:30,844 --> 00:01:32,280
Her drawing is really excellent.

21
00:01:33,559 --> 00:01:42,410
B consults with 3D modelers, effects artists, animators, and so on, and faithfully does the work assigned to him as a member of the game development team.

22
00:01:42,411 --> 00:01:44,890
He finds making the game enjoyable.

23
00:01:45,289 --> 00:01:47,450
His drawing is ordinary.

24
00:01:47,451 --> 00:01:52,090
Here, of course, B would have a sense of responsibility as a professional.

25
00:01:52,094 --> 00:01:56,570
If it were me, I'd give B a higher performance score.

26
00:01:56,571 --> 00:01:59,450
Naturally, his salary will go up.

27
00:01:59,451 --> 00:02:05,530
In the end, a professional's sense of responsibility is directly tied to money, which is the very meaning of being a professional.

28
00:02:05,531 --> 00:02:14,170
In a structure where someone commissions you, you draw for them, and you get paid, working alone as a professional is meaningless.

29
00:02:14,730 --> 00:02:19,450
Because drawing to fit the client's needs comes first.

30
00:02:19,451 --> 00:02:21,530
You can't just draw whatever you want.

31
00:02:22,940 --> 00:02:28,110
If you want to succeed by drawing only what you want to draw, you'd have to become an independent artist.

32
00:02:28,111 --> 00:02:36,830
You'd actively promote on social media, sell your work somewhere like Patreon, or hold exhibitions.

33
00:02:36,831 --> 00:02:41,390
But then why do people who just want to draw well try to get hired by game companies?

34
00:02:42,940 --> 00:02:45,790
What's your goal?

35
00:02:45,791 --> 00:02:48,030
Is it that you want to make games well?

36
00:02:48,031 --> 00:02:50,750
Is it that you want to become an artist?

37
00:02:50,751 --> 00:02:56,990
We've talked a lot about the purpose of drawing, but we've never talked about the goal.

38
00:02:56,991 --> 00:03:01,790
Actually, my goal was to become famous through my drawings and make my name known.

39
00:03:01,870 --> 00:03:03,950
Because of a certain rapper, it's gotten quite difficult.

40
00:03:05,150 --> 00:03:10,590
I was a concept artist, but when I got home, I only practiced illustration.

41
00:03:10,591 --> 00:03:13,490
The reason most of my personal work is illustrations is connected to that.

42
00:03:14,535 --> 00:03:18,850
I couldn't really say that drawing concept art was my life goal.

43
00:03:20,055 --> 00:03:28,800
I feel a bit sorry saying this to people who work hard because they genuinely love concept art, but the reason I got hired in concept art is that it was the conventional path.

44
00:03:29,765 --> 00:03:41,760
However, once I started working in a company and saw my drawings get turned into 3D, move, make sounds, have effects attached, and run around in a game, I gradually started to get interested.

45
00:03:41,761 --> 00:03:47,801
As the work grew familiar and I thought, "I'd like to release a game someday," ten years have passed doing that work.

46
00:03:48,920 --> 00:04:02,010
My story has gotten a bit long. Anyway, my goal wasn't to make my name known through games, but through drawings, so it was awkward for me to call myself a game developer.

47
00:04:02,011 --> 00:04:06,250
I think there are many people with the same concern as me.

48
00:04:06,251 --> 00:04:13,530
I know that most of the classmates taking this concept art class are aiming for game concept art.

49
00:04:13,531 --> 00:04:16,570
Those who aren't, just listen with one ear and let it go in one ear and out the other.

50
00:04:16,571 --> 00:04:22,290
Seriously think about whether you're going into game concept art because you really love making games.

51
00:04:23,855 --> 00:04:27,320
Actually, even if you think about it, not much will change.

52
00:04:27,321 --> 00:04:36,840
Because in Korea, the game industry makes far more money than other industries, and among the jobs you can get through drawing, the salary is also good.

53
00:04:38,140 --> 00:04:45,390
After about a year or two of working yourself to the bone, a time will definitely come when you feel confused about your identity as a drawing person.

54
00:04:45,391 --> 00:04:52,510
That is, whether I want to succeed as an artist or succeed as a game developer.

55
00:04:52,511 --> 00:04:55,310
What's your goal?

56
00:04:55,311 --> 00:05:01,470
If you've been studying vaguely because you wanted to draw well, from now on, think about it once.

57
00:05:01,471 --> 00:05:06,350
If you're going to be a professional, then if you intend to be a professional, you need to have a clear goal.

58
00:05:07,800 --> 00:05:18,090
The growth stages for those with a game-developer mindset will progress in order of concept artist, team lead, art director, and project director.

59
00:05:18,091 --> 00:05:25,450
Each role has different work, so you can't compare their importance, but if you're talking about the hierarchy, it'd be this order.

60
00:05:26,250 --> 00:05:30,890
The higher the position, the less often you'll draw.

61
00:05:30,891 --> 00:05:34,970
By the time you reach art director or project director, you basically don't draw anymore.

62
00:05:34,971 --> 00:05:36,170
You just give orders.

63
00:05:37,655 --> 00:05:44,320
The growth stage for those with an artist mindset would be becoming an illustrator specialist.

64
00:05:44,321 --> 00:05:45,520
It's very simple.

65
00:05:46,110 --> 00:05:50,160
And it will be a very, very hard road.

66
00:05:50,161 --> 00:05:58,240
Making money from your drawings on their own, without going through a platform or medium like games, is even harder.

67
00:05:58,241 --> 00:06:02,880
How much money have you spent in your life buying art?

68
00:06:02,881 --> 00:06:06,521
There are probably many who have never bought a piece of art.

69
00:06:06,680 --> 00:06:17,320
At most, you'd buy an illustration that's collectible in a game, buy one at an exhibition, or buy a printed illustration at a festival.

70
00:06:17,321 --> 00:06:21,560
In other words, it's hard to open a consumer's wallet under normal circumstances.

71
00:06:21,561 --> 00:06:24,760
So people step into the industry.

72
00:06:24,761 --> 00:06:28,920
There are industries like games, webtoons, and movies.

73
00:06:28,921 --> 00:06:34,641
Anyway, this illustrator profession has far less demand in the industry than concept artist.

74
00:06:35,240 --> 00:06:37,960
It's a red ocean within a red ocean.

75
00:06:37,961 --> 00:06:42,920
So usually, many people start out as a concept artist like me.

76
00:06:44,225 --> 00:06:48,350
Which path would you all choose?

77
00:06:48,351 --> 00:06:52,590
I said that drawing reasonably well isn't that hard.

78
00:06:52,591 --> 00:06:55,070
But the path as a professional is hard.

79
00:06:56,375 --> 00:07:02,720
Think about how much harder you want to work and to what level you want to rise.

80
00:07:02,721 --> 00:07:09,920
If you want to draw moderately, get a moderate salary, and live a moderate life, that's not bad either.

81
00:07:09,921 --> 00:07:14,560
I don't want to weigh in on your subjective happiness.

82
00:07:14,561 --> 00:07:21,840
If you want to rise to a higher level and compete with people in the top ranks, then a rugged path unfolds again.

83
00:07:23,130 --> 00:07:29,420
From here on, the difference in achievement will determine your value as a professional.

84
00:07:29,421 --> 00:07:39,340
As a concept artist, the best achievement would be the game or movie you're like the concept for becoming a success in the entertainment market.

85
00:07:40,330 --> 00:07:50,860
As an art artist, I think the best achievement would be communicating directly with consumers and being recognized, without going through an entertainment medium.

86
00:07:50,861 --> 00:07:51,861
Communication and recognition.

87
00:07:52,340 --> 00:07:55,741
I said earlier that I focus on raising your value.

88
00:07:56,235 --> 00:08:03,380
But I can only show you the way; to raise your value, it's important to promote yourself.

89
00:08:03,381 --> 00:08:07,780
You have to publicize your drawings and let people know that you worked on the concept art.

90
00:08:08,480 --> 00:08:11,300
No one is going to package you for free.

91
00:08:11,301 --> 00:08:14,980
You need to focus on packaging yourself well and raising your value.

92
00:08:16,020 --> 00:08:20,500
That's why I actively encourage artists to be active on social media.

93
00:08:21,520 --> 00:08:23,680
Don't draw in hiding.

94
00:08:23,681 --> 00:08:34,720
Professionals in other creative fields use every means available to promote themselves, but artists in particular tend to draw in hiding.

95
00:08:34,721 --> 00:08:37,440
You say doing your job well at the company is enough?

96
00:08:37,441 --> 00:08:40,320
The company doesn't care about your value.

97
00:08:40,321 --> 00:08:42,559
You have to survive on your own.

98
00:08:42,560 --> 00:08:45,600
I'm absolutely not saying to be negligent about your company work.

99
00:08:45,601 --> 00:08:48,320
You have to separate your studies from your life.

100
00:08:48,321 --> 00:08:49,440
If you're an illustrator,

101
00:08:49,555 --> 00:08:58,540
If you're a concept artist, find the time to draw personal illustrations or personal concept work and post them on social media.

102
00:08:58,541 --> 00:09:02,580
That'll promote you automatically and raise your value.

103
00:09:06,940 --> 00:09:10,350
I've been talking about theories quite broadly.

104
00:09:10,351 --> 00:09:13,390
Let me also talk about something a bit more blunt.

105
00:09:13,439 --> 00:09:17,310
The subtitle of this session is how to survive as a professional, right?

106
00:09:17,311 --> 00:09:19,230
What does it mean to be "alive"?

107
00:09:19,231 --> 00:09:22,870
It means a state where you're exercising influence.

108
00:09:23,100 --> 00:09:25,470
Why do you need to survive?

109
00:09:25,471 --> 00:09:32,190
Well, there are nice words like "duty" and the like, but what modern society needs most and what really hits home is money.

110
00:09:32,191 --> 00:09:35,710
It's about making a living, so how much do professionals actually earn?

111
00:09:35,735 --> 00:09:40,030
Let me talk about how much you earn on which path.

112
00:09:40,031 --> 00:09:43,870
I'm in the game industry, so I'll tell you based on the game industry.

113
00:09:45,125 --> 00:09:54,041
First, if you get hired as a game concept artist, it varies by company, but the starting salary is as low as around fifteen million won per year.

114
00:09:54,080 --> 00:09:58,480
And the higher the rank and role, the more you get paid.

115
00:09:58,481 --> 00:10:07,040
In Korea, for character concept artists, the annual salary is usually capped at around eighty million to one hundred million won.

116
00:10:07,041 --> 00:10:12,201
To go above that, you have to climb to team lead, art director, or project director.

117
00:10:13,285 --> 00:10:17,680
For illustrators, I've almost never seen game companies hire them separately.

118
00:10:18,695 --> 00:10:22,160
Concept artists do it in parallel, or something like that.

119
00:10:22,161 --> 00:10:25,920
So you can consider the salary level to be the same.

120
00:10:25,921 --> 00:10:30,880
However, if you work as a freelancer, the cost side is different.

121
00:10:30,881 --> 00:10:50,970
Usually, concept artists price their work around one million to two and a half million won per concept, while illustrations have a huge range, from a million won per piece up to over ten million, depending on the workload and the artist's value.

122
00:10:50,971 --> 00:10:54,250
As a freelancer, where do you get your commissions from?

123
00:10:54,251 --> 00:10:57,050
You hear talk about connections a lot.

124
00:10:57,051 --> 00:11:00,810
Like that saying that getting a job is all about connections, that kind of talk.

125
00:11:00,811 --> 00:11:03,050
I'm kind of an introvert, so I don't have connections.

126
00:11:03,051 --> 00:11:07,770
In my case, I was contacted almost entirely through social media.

127
00:11:07,771 --> 00:11:09,850
I said it's important to promote yourself.

128
00:11:10,640 --> 00:11:15,940
Diligently post your drawings on your social media, like arranging flowers in a vase.

129
00:11:15,941 --> 00:11:24,020
Once your portfolio flower bouquet becomes beautiful, the bees and butterflies will come carrying money pollen.

130
00:11:24,021 --> 00:11:28,580
If you do freelance work, the speed of your hands also becomes incredibly important.

131
00:11:28,581 --> 00:11:32,500
Because if your work speed is fast, you can take on more commissions in the same amount of time.

132
00:11:34,070 --> 00:11:46,680
If you're an illustrator, you can post your unreleased works on a crowdfunding site like Patreon and get support, or post work videos on YouTube to earn income.

133
00:11:46,681 --> 00:11:54,360
The range is enormous, from people who are famous like celebrities to people who have no supporters at all.

134
00:11:54,361 --> 00:12:01,560
You have to pay attention not just to the art, but also to fan management, video management, and many other elements.

135
00:12:01,561 --> 00:12:02,600
I haven't done it, so

136
00:12:02,720 --> 00:12:09,561
I don't fully understand that effort, but just drawing is already hard enough, so I think it's amazing.

137
00:12:10,545 --> 00:12:14,010
If you have the ability, you could also teach.

138
00:12:14,011 --> 00:12:20,131
There are offline classes, and after the COVID pandemic, online classes have become the trend.

139
00:12:20,545 --> 00:12:23,180
This is an industry secret, so don't spread it around.

140
00:12:23,181 --> 00:12:32,700
The split varies by academy, but it ranges from 60% for the academy and 40% for the instructor, to 20% for the academy and 80% for the instructor.

141
00:12:32,701 --> 00:12:40,181
Online courses are free from rent, computer and machine costs, electricity bills, and so on, so the ratio is a bit better.

142
00:12:41,320 --> 00:12:44,881
In my case, I worked as an academy instructor for about four months.

143
00:12:45,320 --> 00:12:56,120
Because I'm really bad at multitasking and not great at speaking, I don't know what the students thought of me back then, but I feel I wasn't able to teach them properly, so I'm really sorry.

144
00:12:56,121 --> 00:13:00,760
Aside from drawing skill, I think teaching ability is really important.

145
00:13:00,761 --> 00:13:09,440
In Class101, I teach by recording, so I can give you more information and demonstrations than I did in those four months, which is great.

146
00:13:11,205 --> 00:13:21,600
Anyway, people like this who speak well, draw well, and explain well don't even need to work at a company; they make a living working only at academies, earning money without being jealous of anyone.

147
00:13:21,601 --> 00:13:24,080
These days there are a lot of multi-talented people.

148
00:13:24,190 --> 00:13:26,880
Those kinds of people seem to do well.

149
00:13:26,881 --> 00:13:27,881
I'm envious.

150
00:13:28,700 --> 00:13:31,230
Learning English would also be good.

151
00:13:31,231 --> 00:13:36,510
I mainly receive commissions through ArtStation, and they all come in English.

152
00:13:36,590 --> 00:13:43,031
These days translation tools are quite good, but if you learn one foreign language, it'll be a real weapon.

153
00:13:44,150 --> 00:13:48,600
This time we talked about living your life as a professional.

154
00:13:48,601 --> 00:13:52,040
There are so, so many capable people in the world.

155
00:13:52,041 --> 00:14:02,280
Think about whether you'll compete with those people just moderately, fall behind moderately, and survive, or push forward and take the lead.

156
00:14:02,281 --> 00:14:10,280
If you want to take the lead, also think about what competitiveness you'll build compared to those people.

157
00:14:10,281 --> 00:14:13,240
What your weapon is and how you'll use it.

158
00:14:13,300 --> 00:14:16,941
I hope this becomes a chance for you to seriously think about it.

159
00:14:17,655 --> 00:14:21,480
Next time, we'll have a Q&A session.
